Z||000010||Philphine||10-08-2000||03:52 PM||||buy the pair if you have to and make the other a "powerbulge" style hood scoop. sort of like an elipse or talon (vent facing rearward obviously) the contour is close enough that you can almost screw it on the hood with no mods, depending on how well it flexes (it's really close just setting it on the hood), and if you glassed it it would mold in no problem.<P>rock or whoever buys the molds, if you made a similar scoop about1/2-1" (that part may not even be nessesary) narrower as it sticks out from the car and about 3" (top to bottom as it sits on car) wider you could put them in either spot and have all matching vents/scoops. had to stop writing to check one sitting on the back ('84 with closed side panels) it sits back there too and almost matches contourwise. all the spots seem close enough that you could make them for the sides and someone buying them could set them in the other spots too see if they want to adapt them to fit. you'd still just need one left/right pair of molds.   <BR>uh....you don't have to pay me for that idea. just send me about 3 sets if you do it and we'll call it square.||||reg||1
Z||000011||rockcrawl||10-08-2000||05:59 PM||||fropuff, If you're going to glass them in, maybe you can use one without the rear flange. I have some here without flanges, but if you want them, you'll need to buy both. Also I'm not sure how well the fiberglass resin will bond to the plastic panels, if at all. You might want to check into that before starting. I would be worried about cracks because the plastic flexes a lot more than the fibreglass. They are approximately 13.5" long X 5.5" wide X 2" tall.<P>Phil, nice idea, but of course I dont have time to do any more design work. My buddy said he wants a pair for the hood of his Jeep!<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
